  "text_sanskrit": "न मे विदुः सुरगणाः प्रभवं न महर्षयः । अहमादिर्हि देवानां महर्षीणां च सर्वशः ॥ १०-२ ॥",
  "itrans": "na me viduH suragaNAH prabhavaM na maharShayaH . ahamAdirhi devAnAM maharShINA.n cha sarvashaH .. 10-2 ..",
  "translation": "Neither the multitudes of the gods nor the great Rishis, know My glory or birth or creation for I am the first (cause of the gods, of the great Rishis, of all. (2)\nV. The Prabhava in the text is to be taken in three senses: (1) The gods and the Rishis cannot fully comprehend My glory; (2) They cannot think of My birth; (3) They cannot be the witnesses of My creative activity for they subsequently come into existence. My creation: the creation (of the world) which lies in My power. Mistake should not be made by thinking that the Lord also might have origin, though unknown like His glory, or the beginning of the world. It should be clearly seen that the Lord has no beginning as surely as His glory does exist or as surely as the origination of the world is a fact. The Lord having no origin, the gods and Rishis do not know His origin. The reason is given in the latter half. Of the Gods, &c.: includes men also; i.e., of the whole world. All things are My creation and there is none to create Me. Asat or nothingness cannot be said to create anything, nor is it consistent to speak of My creating Myself (vide Sutra-)\nA. It is a glorious attribute of the Lord that to know Him is the surest way to release from mundane bondage.",
